#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228783 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6329ab32-968f-5041-b2e3-12dde408968a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Implicit Euler Method"}] :question/id #uuid "185f4059-c0d2-59a0-932b-d1152c407db6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21317" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = y^3 - x\\,y, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x = 1,$ the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = (y_\\text{new})^3 - 3y_\\text{new} - 1$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + 1$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 - 1$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new}$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new} - y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}-1 \\\\  & =3{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228784 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "866481fb-c460-5a39-a7a0-84b64d8bd673" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Implicit Euler Method: One Step"}] :question/id #uuid "2e45e65d-ffd1-5418-9445-eee71cb36ded" :question/math-academy-id "e-21319" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy + y^3, \\qquad y(1.5)=-0.3\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1.5 = 0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1.5 + 0.5 = 2.$ The old $y$ value is $y = -0.3.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+(2-1){y}_{\\text{new}}-0.3 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-0.3$ to solve $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 1.5(y_\\text{new})^2 + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{0.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^3 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 0.3}{1.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + 1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1.5) = -0.3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =-0.3 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈0.240529 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.288849 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.288050\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 0.29,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6329ab32-968f-5041-b2e3-12dde408968a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Implicit Euler Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f742b2b-722d-5194-a318-86387c81d136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333175} {:answer/id #uuid "443e1ec1-6ca0-57e5-9c73-e9d62a9dcdaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333176} {:answer/id #uuid "3704e48f-b5f7-596a-b729-98159c2867b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333177} {:answer/id #uuid "834e9bd6-d7c7-5862-96f5-1d2864cef316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333178} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc55c7e-de33-5030-b664-caa5883d51d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fc55c7e-de33-5030-b664-caa5883d51d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333179} :answer-field/id #uuid "1295455d-c417-5479-af9f-22c212196b10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "106b38b1-7560-51f1-b255-f587828e56e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-325128" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-x,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1+2 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "866481fb-c460-5a39-a7a0-84b64d8bd673" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Implicit Euler Method: One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d860c910-d87c-5fb0-b32f-65fb1a08f0c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333180} {:answer/id #uuid "0437b064-3f8e-5d28-ae4e-b572684a35a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d860c910-d87c-5fb0-b32f-65fb1a08f0c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333180} :answer-field/id #uuid "d42eb830-acd8-5822-a929-9a9d6b943d81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bfc747b-4f72-569b-afba-2705d78bce41" :question/math-academy-id "q-325137" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-{y}^{3},\\,y(0.5)=1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-2}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.5)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.2 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.179747 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.179509\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.18,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6329ab32-968f-5041-b2e3-12dde408968a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Implicit Euler Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ce083c1-be74-5a0d-a02c-428c12f7cf26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-y+0.5\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333184} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ca7d8d-84a1-52e9-904d-83daea9a8a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25{y}^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333185} {:answer/id #uuid "0f9b3f31-f964-5fbf-9731-92a14a77966f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-y+0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333186} {:answer/id #uuid "208f90b4-14ef-50a6-9631-d7ba0f9b811f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=-\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}+y+0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333187} {:answer/id #uuid "be939bc7-0b4a-5fb9-88bc-634185230620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be939bc7-0b4a-5fb9-88bc-634185230620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333188} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6070c0-2619-51ea-9871-1b2db9339a1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4896342-4a82-5ff5-9543-c3f3560c7f74" :question/math-academy-id "q-333102" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=0.25,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **general form** of $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ in terms of the new values ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and the current value $y?$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side to define $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {{-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.25$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6329ab32-968f-5041-b2e3-12dde408968a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Implicit Euler Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4719df-a81e-56eb-9020-3b51de065f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333191} {:answer/id #uuid "0d74821f-593f-5e01-914a-e5e89c7b1c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+0.875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333192} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a6202e-0b68-542c-9ad7-dcc2dd5476c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333193} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c67570-58aa-59ab-8b75-a85e278c411e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333194} {:answer/id #uuid "f3746890-a177-5cc3-b590-8fc5a9f03807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8c67570-58aa-59ab-8b75-a85e278c411e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333194} :answer-field/id #uuid "e864b392-5ae2-5d2b-903d-5a25e3c1398a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4eb3553e-0592-5fd5-a3bf-e18a3b33cb12" :question/math-academy-id "q-333115" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=0.5,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1+0.5=1.5.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2+0.5(1.5)^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2+1.125 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.875.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6329ab32-968f-5041-b2e3-12dde408968a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Implicit Euler Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5968804-4e43-5b9e-9907-1c936edb34a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333198} {:answer/id #uuid "f69f2081-3706-52cf-8bca-0cd4f1128fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333199} {:answer/id #uuid "3f20fb39-a5e4-58fe-9ff7-ae201b9dbc3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333200} {:answer/id #uuid "290104ce-726c-50f5-b2a9-830591f06255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333201} {:answer/id #uuid "cd48ae99-66a4-5f14-977f-63f38e2aa3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd48ae99-66a4-5f14-977f-63f38e2aa3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333202} :answer-field/id #uuid "2366db65-d988-5a29-8ef6-a260641ec4e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "987f082e-c646-530c-a077-62628daa005f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333118" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\,{y}^{2}-y,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=0.5,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+0.5=1.5.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5\\cdot 1.5\\,({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6329ab32-968f-5041-b2e3-12dde408968a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Implicit Euler Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9c272c2-56d1-5e8d-ba52-600af580fa86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333205} {:answer/id #uuid "41002980-522a-549b-b7df-456acca54463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333206} {:answer/id #uuid "3996c3a0-0355-5992-a89e-de62c367ba1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333207} {:answer/id #uuid "e6447de1-e49e-5918-9c46-a6aec7d1ef47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333208} {:answer/id #uuid "50d84280-e30e-5c4b-b8b7-856ba18bf485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3996c3a0-0355-5992-a89e-de62c367ba1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333207} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a8cf68c-b1a1-5a71-a501-18b7dbee563b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecd7ba84-c62d-5665-b104-2afa83226b1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333120" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-2-(2)^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-2-4 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "866481fb-c460-5a39-a7a0-84b64d8bd673" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Implicit Euler Method: One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c4f685-3868-5166-8405-5afc585fab92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333212} {:answer/id #uuid "a9303163-f03f-5ed7-bdf1-8af761f3c95a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03c4f685-3868-5166-8405-5afc585fab92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333212} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7f39006-8d91-5d25-9c47-6d88d4247237" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5748741-7fd4-5804-824d-c170b9b9a6e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-333250" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-{y}^{2},\\,y(0.5)=0.7\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5(1){y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.7 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+0.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=0.7$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})+0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-0.7}{({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+0.5}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.5)=0.7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =0.7 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈0.7875 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.7845 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.7845\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈0.78,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "866481fb-c460-5a39-a7a0-84b64d8bd673" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Implicit Euler Method: One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2936ca28-5262-5d62-a6af-0ff25413143e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2936ca28-5262-5d62-a6af-0ff25413143e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333216} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e7b9149-4b67-5e4a-b577-ed45ac683e65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba6a97bd-3dcd-5632-8f7b-141d5415a286" :question/math-academy-id "q-333263" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.5).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.5,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.5-0=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+0.5=0.5.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5(0.5)({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-0.5(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.25(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.125}{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈0.916667 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.915476 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.915476\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.5)≈0.92,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "866481fb-c460-5a39-a7a0-84b64d8bd673" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Implicit Euler Method: One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf65f1c9-4290-588a-aad4-f1be617c9744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf65f1c9-4290-588a-aad4-f1be617c9744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333219} :answer-field/id #uuid "72c06340-7737-55a2-8f39-11fac3e736eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48eba903-10e9-5e4d-bd8f-9f12d76f76d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333282" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.4).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.4,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.4-0=0.4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(3({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.4$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-1.2({x}_{\\text{new}}).\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+0.4=0.4.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-1.2(0.4) \\\\  & =0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.8({y}_{\\text{new}})+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.4(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.48}{0.8(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.044444 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.044014 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.044014\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.4)≈1.04,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "866481fb-c460-5a39-a7a0-84b64d8bd673" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Implicit Euler Method: One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b824b442-fd06-58d2-aec7-a720b76a0f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b824b442-fd06-58d2-aec7-a720b76a0f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333222} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7422f1f-17a8-5100-8df5-968d1a58b2d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "443b0f9b-97de-5973-ba1f-c36595b2443c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333286" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-{y}^{2},\\,y(0.4)=1.2\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.9).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.9)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.9,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.9-0.4=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}}).\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.4+0.5=0.9.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.2-(0.9) \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1.2$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-2.1}{({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.4)=1.2,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1.2 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.281818 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.280351 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.280351\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.9)≈1.28,$ rounded to two decimal places."}]}
